[USSR_WW2.FullName]
The Great Patriotic War
[USSR_WW2.Description]
[USSR_WW2.Epilogue]
The Great Patriotic War of 1941-1945 ended with the complete victory of the Soviet people over Nazi Germany. Fascism was eliminated in Germany itself and in a number of European states. In a hard, bloody struggle, the Soviet people defended their national sovereignty, defended their homeland. Having defeated the shock forces of world reaction, the Soviet Union and its Armed Forces carried out a historic liberation mission in Europe and Asia, made a decisive contribution to the salvation of European and world civilization.

The victory in the Second World War was achieved by the joint efforts of the countries participating in the Anti-Hitler Coalition. The contribution of the Western Allies, who defeated and captured 176 divisions, is significant. But it was the Soviet people who bore the brunt of the struggle. For almost four years, the Soviet-German front chained to itself the bulk of the forces and means of fascist Germany. From 190 to 270 of the most combat-ready divisions of the fascist bloc were simultaneously operating against the Soviet troops, i.e., more than 3/4 of their total number. 607 enemy divisions were defeated and captured on the Soviet-German front. The total human losses of the German armed forces in World War II reached 13.4 million people, on the Soviet-German front — 10 million people. The irretrievable losses of Germany and its allies on the Soviet-German front amounted to 8,649.5 thousand people. During the war, Soviet troops destroyed and captured more than 75% of all enemy weapons and military equipment.

The victory went to the Soviet Union at a high price. The total human losses of the USSR during the war amounted to 26.6 million people. Among them, servicemen and partisans killed in battle and died of wounds, died of hunger and disease, peaceful Soviet citizens killed by bombing and shelling, prisoners of war shot by punishers and tortured in concentration camps, as well as party, Komsomol and Soviet activists. The irretrievable demographic losses of the Soviet Armed Forces during the Great Patriotic War of 1941-1945 and the Soviet-Japanese War of 1945 amounted to 8 million 668.4 thousand servicemen. At the same time, the Red Army and Navy lost 8 million 509.3 thousand, internal troops — 97.7 thousand, border troops — 61.4 thousand people. Sanitary losses according to reports from the troops amounted to 18 million 344.1 thousand people (including wounded, shell—shocked — 15 million 205.6 thousand, sick — 3 million 47.8 thousand, frostbitten - 90.9 thousand). The Soviet Armed Forces suffered heavy losses in operations to liberate the peoples of Europe and Asia.

[R002.FullName]
Barbarossa Begins
[R002.Description]
The start of the campaign against the USSR – or as the Russians call it the Great Patriotic War –was preceded with a complex political situation. After capturing France, Hitler was facing a strategic dilemma: either attack England, which was already in the state of war against Germany, or go eastwards breaking the non-aggression pact with the Soviet Union. Stalin, sure that he was on an inevitable path to conflict with Germany, never believed things would develop so quickly, right up to the invasion itself. Hitler and his generals chose the war against the USSR, which at first seemed a good decision – the German army managed to carry out a quick and large-scale offensive – but finally turned out to be the end of the Thousand-year Reich. On June 14, 1941 the German command met at Imperial Chancellery for the last prewar briefing. Turning toward Herman Goering, Hitler noted: "This will be... our hardest struggle..."

On June 22, before dawn broke, the Nazis started a massive offensive across an enormous front stretching from Moldavia to the Baltic. Although the Soviet defense commissar sent out the last-minute defense warning to the frontiers many detachments never received it due to the successful actions of the German intelligence that sabotaged the communications. The Soviets fought bravely; although many units were caught in encirclements they fought to the last soldier. Overall the situation at the fronts in the beginning of the war could be described as catastrophic. As soon as one week after the start of the invasion the Nazis took over the bigger part of the Baltic, captured Minsk and surrounded the Russians at Grodno and Belostok. 

Kiev – the capital of ancient Russia – was held as one of the top priorities by the German command. The quick capturing of the city by the Army Group South, according to the Barbarossa plan, would considerably weaken the entire country both from the military and psychological viewpoints. Already on the first day of war the 1st tank group commanded by von Kleist began the advance to Kiev. Stalin too understood the importance of this strategic direction, which was why he sent the soon-to-become-legendary Marshal Grigory Zhukov over to carry out the general coordination. Under the joint command of Zhukov and Front Commander the Russians went into the first full-scale tank battle against the Germans in the area of Lutsk, Brody and Rovno. As a result of this battle, which involved over a thousand of tanks on both sides, the German advance was slowed down. One of the greatest successes was the counterattack of the 9th and 19th mechanized corps at Rovno on June 26, 1941. The entire fight only involved tanks and a small number of Motorized Infantry detachments. They managed to cut off the Lutsk-Rovno road, and the troops of the 43rd tank division took Dubno, although not before the larger part of the German 11th Tank Division left it continuing the advance to the east.

[R003.FullName]
Surrounded 
[R003.Description]
Over the three months that passed since the beginning of the war the situation for the Soviets has gone from bad to worse. The enemy occupied the larger part of Ukraine, they were advancing to Leningrad and Kiev had to be given up.

The only relative success was the battle of Smolensk where the German Army Group Center lost up to 250 thousand men and was forced to stop the advance for several weeks. Near Yelnya the Russian counterattack even managed to push the Nazis back. One of the key objectives of August-September of 1941 as set by the German command was the taking and reinforcing the positions in the south of the USSR, namely the Crimea and the coal-rich areas of Donbass. The first several months of the campaign taught the Soviet command that they had to build their defenses around the suppression of tank detachments, which the enemy used for their blitzkrieg attacks and encirclements. These months also taught the German command that the war in Russia was indeed going to be a very tough nut to crack. They were facing the risk of a lengthy campaign, which is why by October of the same year the group of armies Center was given a new objective – taking Moscow by winter at any and all costs. 

Guderian’s breakthroughs at the Bryansk and western Fronts (September. to October 1941), which were completely unexpected by the Soviet command, became the beginning of Operation Typhoon. The capital of the USSR was facing 77 enemy Divisions numbering up to 800,000 men, 1,700 tanks and vehicles, 14,000 field guns and mortars and 1,390 planes. The Supreme Command Staff was beginning to worry about the Mozhaisk area, which had not been properly fortified. The scattered and defeated troops of the Reserve and Western Fronts were regrouped into one Western Front, which was tasked with defending the capital. However the detachments that were defending the area did not retreat on time. On October 7 enemy tank groups met in the vicinity of Vyazma in the rear of the Soviet forces, once again making a huge "pocket" and surrounding over one million Soviet troops. The scale of this encirclement was so large that it took 28 German Divisions and quite a bit of time to try to destroy the trapped Soviet forces. 
 
The Russian troops covering Moscow in the Gzhatsk and Maloyaroslavets directions were making a stand but had to retreat after all.


[R004.FullName]
Moscow is behind us
[R004.Description]
In October of 1941 the enemy was advancing onto Moscow from all sides. Hitler demanded that the Soviet capital had to be taken as soon as possible. On the western side in the areas of Mozhaisk and Volokolamsk the frontline was just 100 km away from the city. In the southwest too the German detachments were advancing quite successfully. 

After the brisk capturing of the town of Orel by Guderian, the next target on the way to Moscow was Tula. This town, which had been the center of Russian arms making since times eternal, was now right on the frontline. The arms making plants were quickly evacuated and the town itself was turned into a fortress – with antitank hedgehogs and militia detachments. The German command considered Tula a perspective direction – mainly because of the earlier successes – and was demanding an offensive. 

In this situation the Russian Staff relocated the 5th Airborne Corps over to Tula. They were joined by the 34th Border Guard Regiment, which was earlier responsible for the rear guard of the Bryansk Front. The Soviets defended well doing everything possible to stop the enemy march against Moscow. Under Mtsensk the Russian command deployed the 1st Guard Rifle Regiment under the command of Major-General Lelyushenko, and sent over 11th Tank Brigade commanded by Colonel Katukov. Battling was especially severe in the vicinity of Mtsensk, Likhvin, Chern’, and Teploye. The Soviet troops put up a stubborn resistance, making scarce counterattacks and bearing more and more losses every day. 

At the same time life was not much easier for the Germans. In his "Soldier’s Memories" Guderian notes that his armored materiel at Tula was constantly lacking fuel, while the advance overall went very slowly because of the meltdown and broken roads. The soldiers were suffering because of the absence of warm uniforms (caused by the optimism of the General Staff concerning the expected length of the USSR campaign) and lice. The retreating Soviet troops were making sure to destroy all the bridges. Strong blizzards hit on November 3, which stopped the accumulation of mud but also brought a whole new world of suffering to the infantry. 

As a result of the persistent heroic defense of the Russians, the exhausting of the reserves of Army Group Center, mistakes in the German supply communications and difficult weather conditions, the advance was stopped. Tula did not surrender to the Germans.

[R005.FullName]
Heroic Deeds
[R005.Description]
In October of 1941 severe fighting broke out in the northwest of Russia, near the encircled city of Leningrad. In the southwest, the Romanians attacked Odessa. Taganrog fell, and the Germans were now approaching Kharkov and Belgorod, while the 11th German army was preparing to advance into the Crimea. However, the main attention of both the Russian and the German Commands was concentrated on Moscow.

After the tragic encirclement by Vyazma, where the trapped forces of the Western Front were heroically drawing the German fire against themselves for ten consequent days, the defense of the Western approaches to Moscow were still being prepared. Eleven Rifle Divisions, 16 Tank Brigades and over 40 Artillery Regiments were being pulled to Moscow from the rear and from other fronts. By mid-October the main approaches to Moscow were covered by a force totaling 90,000 soldiers. Three Rifle Divisions and two Tank Divisions were being transferred from the Far East. Hundreds of trains with reinforcements were rolling day and night, only stopping to change engines. The most dangerous areas were covered by troops under command of the most experienced battlefield commanders: Rokossovsky was in charge of the Volokolamsk section, Lelyushenko covered the Mozhaisk road, where he was wounded and soon replaced by Govorov. In the meantime the Germans regrouped and resumed their advance. 

The 316th Rifle Division under the command of General Panfilov was charged to stop the enemy at the Volokolamsk road. The last train with reinforcements from Krestsy and Borovichi arrived at the Volokolamsk station on October 11, 1941. No real defenses had been prepared, nor had there been any reserves to rely on. The division deployed along the Ruza-Lotoshino line and began building fortifications at the most probable directions of enemy attacks. General Panfilov was certain that the Germans once again would leave the main assault to their tanks. The exhaustive fighting never stopped and by late October the defensive line of the Panfilov Division stretched for 20 kilometers – from the Dubosekovo intersection to the village of Teryaevo. Having pulled in some reinforcements, the Germans replaced the worn-out divisions with fresh ones, rolled out over 350 tanks and were ready once more for the main assault. On November 16 the advance began. The night fight of the tank destroyer group of the 1075th Regiment under the command of Vassily Klochkov is described in every Russian history schoolbook. For four hours the brave soldiers held back the enemy tanks and infantry. They repulsed several attacks and destroyed 18 German tanks. The majority of these brave Soviet soldiers – including Klochkov – died. On November 17, 1941 the 316th Rifle Division was renamed to the 8th Guard Division and awarded with the Red Banner Order.

[R007.FullName]
Winter Offense 
[R007.Description]
December of 1941 became the nightmare for the Germans near Moscow: the command was trying to push the troops forward but they already had no strength or capability to capture Moscow. The Wehrmacht was not prepared for a winter campaign and the stubborn resistance of the Russians prevented any attempts of breaking the frontlines open. The German tank reserves were exhausted to a mere 1,000 units. A reasonable commander would have retreated back to fortified positions and waited till spring, but Hitler was no reasonable commander. His attention was locked on exterior politics – on December 8 Japan entered the war on the German side, and three days later the Reich declared war against the United States. In the meantime the Russian command was gradually pulling in the reserves from Siberia and the Far East and saving them so far. Exhausting positional fights and small-scale exchanges were happing along the entire length of the Western Russian Front with no major breakthroughs. The German army was standing at the gates of Moscow approaching the city as close as 50 km in the northwestern sector but yet could not take over the capital. Having accumulated the needed amount of reserves on December 6 the Russians began a counter-attack along the front of 1,000 kilometers from Kalinin to Yelts. All in all, the advance for Moscow lasted for over a month. By early January of 1942 the Soviet troops pushed the enemy forces back 100-250 km. Hitler was outraged – it seemed to him that the German forces had sufficient fighting ability; if not to take Moscow then at least to hold their ground. The front commanders on the other hand, in their desire to save their troops, were pulling the divisions further away from Moscow. This kind of action cost von Bock and Guderian their positions, while less-known commanders paid with their lives. 

The January 7th resolution of the Soviet Staff ordered the troops to encircle and destroy the Army Group Center in the area of Rzhev, Vyazma, Yukhnov and Gzhatsk. The operation was tasked to a number of different fronts. On January 8, the troops and forces of the Northwestern and Kalinin Fronts started their advance going deep into the rear of the Center groups of armies in the vicinity of Velikiye Luki. In parallel the forces of the Western Front were engaged in the Sychevsk-Vyazma operation, fighting their way through to Vyazma from the east. The Soviet armies were trapping and defeating the enemy across the entire front.

[R006.FullName]
Lake Seliger
[R006.Description]
In early January 1942 the main battles unfolded around Moscow. The troops and forces of the Western Front were pushing the enemy back across a wide line from Kalinin to Yelts. By the beginning of the year the Soviets liberated the towns of Stalinogorsk, Solnechnogorsk, Yefremov, Klin, Kalinin, Volokolamsk, Kozelsk, Kaluga approaching the rivers of Lama and Ruza on the right side and reaching the Belev – Mtsensk – Verkhoovye line on the left side. As a result of this massive offensive, the Russians advanced 200 km to the west and defeated 38 Nazi Divisions that were the main striking force of the Army Group Center. 

At the same time the overall situation was not that bright for the Russians and the success in Moscow could not be considered decisive. In the south Crimea and Donbass were still occupied by the Germans, the enemy was standing under Rostov and in the north Leningrad was still under a blockade. The situation was still quite shaky, and the forces of the opposing parties had now practically reached a balance in the number of troops, artillery units, tanks and all other relevant indicators. The Soviet resources were fully converted to military purposes, while with every passing day more and more evacuated plants were re-started in the east of the country. This was particularly important since the hostilities were mainly dependant upon the relative economic capacities of the opponents.

In the meantime, Stalin insisted on continuing the advance, as well as developing it across the entire line of the Soviet-German Front. In the north the Soviet troops approached Lake Seliger and the town of Ostashkov. After that the forces of the Kalinin and Western Fronts were given a new objective: jointly with the armies of the Northwestern and Bryansk Fronts encircle and destroy the bulk of the Army Group Center. However the available troops and materiel were obviously insufficient to achieve the set objectives and the success of this advance was very limited. The Northwestern Front took part in the operation with its 34th, 3rd, and 4th strike armies and the 22nd and 39th regular armies. Obviously, before the arrival of the reinforcements and starting the advance, the recently recaptured positions had to be held.

[R010.FullName]
Strong at Heart
[R010.Description]
Following the numerous successes of the winter campaign, the situation at the fronts in the summer of 1943 was favorable for the Soviets. The German command tried to recapture the initiative. In order to do that they devised the Zeitler plan, which envisaged a maneuver around the Kursk brow of the Central and Voronezh Fronts, a breakthrough of both faces of the front and subsequent encircling and destruction of the Russian forces. The operation became known under the codename of Citadel. Zeitler had especially high hopes in the new models of German tanks, which in his opinion, were to provide the Germans with the decisive advantage. The Tiger however had a series of weaknesses, which made it a lot less promising than the German staff believed. These weaknesses included an insufficient action radius, limited maneuverability and vertical armor plates of the turret, which, although quite thick, were still easier to puncture with a direct shot, than the curved armor of more up-to-date models such as T-34 or Panther. The latter however was truly a gem, and many experts still consider it the best tank of the Second World War. 

However it was not the technical specifications that were to decide the outcome of the Kursk battle. "Warned is armed" – and the Soviet Staff knew about the planned German advance. The Soviet intelligence service did a magnificent job, and after the capturing of one of the German commanders with all the papers detailing the specifics of the offensive, little doubt was left about the planned operation. In period between April and June the Soviets built eight defense frontiers on the Kursk salient reaching up to 300 km in depth. Special attention was paid to creating a strong anti-tank defense. The average mining density in the direction of expected offensive was 1500 AT mines and 1,700 land mines per one kilometer. Several hours before the start of the enemy offensive the Russians unleashed a powerful artillery and aviation counterstrike, as a result of which the enemy suffered substantial losses, besides losing the element of surprise. On the morning of July 5, the Nazis began the advance on the northern face, aiming the main strike at Olkhovatka, where the Russian 13th Army was deployed. However, the offensive choked and the enemy had to shift the main strike in the direction of Ponyri.

[R011.FullName]
Steel Avalanche 
[R011.Description]
The 1942-43 winter campaign was not to the advantage of the Germans. After the stubborn defense at the banks of Volga the Russians defeated the enemy in and around Stalingrad, encircling a large enemy group, rebutted the German offensive at the Caucasus and started a counteroffensive in the south, broke the blockade of Leningrad in the north, and recaptured Kharkov and Kursk. 

Losing the strategic initiative after the bitter defeats during the winter, the German command was now desperately looking for an opportunity to win it back. Germany and its allies were facing a real threat of defeat. In addition to that, parts of the German troops were still engaged in the hostilities in Africa. In the spring of 1943, Hitler’s Chief of General Staff Zeitler offered the plan of attack at the Kursk salient, where the Russians moved their front forward in a broad curve. A double flanking with a simultaneous tank breakthrough was the key strategic maneuver in the beginning of the war – this was the way to accomplish any major encirclement. However, by 1943 the trick became outdated - already in May Marshal Zhukov pointed out the danger of getting the Kursk salient in a German encirclement (at about the same time when the German staff reviewed Zeitler’s plan), and the Soviet command took Zhukov’s warning with all seriousness. Later, but prior to the start of the operation, the Soviet intelligence reported that the summer advance at Kursk was inevitable. In order to accomplish the breakthrough the Germans concentrated up to 50 Divisions, two Tank Brigades, three separate Tank Battalions and eight assault gun squadrons, which were part of the 9th and 2nd Armies of group Center, 4th Tank Army and the Kempf operation group of the Army Group South – all in all, some 900,000 men, up to 10,000 guns and mortars, about 2,700 tanks and assault guns and over 2,000 aircraft. The events that followed the attack at the southern face of the Kursk salient became known as the largest tank battle of WWII.

[R012.FullName]
Zhukov’s Advance
[R012.Description]
After the defeat of the Germans at Kursk the Russians took the strategic initiative once and for all. Advancing across a 2-thousand kilometer front the Red Army was winning one battle after another. In autumn-winter of 1943 the Soviets recaptured Smolensk, Bryansk, Left-bank Ukraine, Novorossiysk, crossed the Dnepr, and liberated Kiev in November. The command also achieved substantial successes in exterior policy: during the summit in Teheran in autumn of 1943 the Allies finally resolved to open the second front. By early 1944 the Nazi army was comprised of 315 Divisions and 10 Brigades. The Soviet-German Front had 198 Divisions and 6 Brigades, 3 Air Fleets, as well as 38 Divisions and 18 Brigades of other Axis countries (in total 4.9 million people, 54,570 guns and mortars, 5,400 tanks and assault guns, and 3,073 planes). 

The enemy defended stubbornly and it took great effort to defeat them. As of the beginning of 1944 the key strategic objective for the USSR was the liberation of the western part of Ukraine, Bellorussia, and the Baltic Republics. Given that objective, the Soviet supreme command planned a full-scale offensive in Ukraine, which was comprised of several smaller attacks: the Zhitomir-Berdichhev, Kirovograd, Korsun’-Shevchenko, Lutsk-Rovno, Nikopol’-Krivoy Rog, Proskurovo-Chernovtsy, Umansko-Botoshanskaya, Bereznegovato-Snigiryovskaya and the Odessa offensives. By April 1944 the major operation was successfully accomplished. As a result of that the Russians now could develop their advance in Polesye moving towards Kovel and Brest. Here, across a 300 plus kilometer long front from the mouth of Ptichya (west of Mozyr) to Rozhishche (north of Lutsk) the Russians had the troops and forces of the 61st army of the Belorussian Front (eight Rifle and six Cavalry Divisions) and three Divisions of the 77th Rifle Corps of the 1st Ukrainian Front’s 13th army. In order to cover the right side of the Soviet strategic group in Ukraine, as well as to develop a separate offensive to Kovel and Brest, on February 17, 1944, a new front was formed in between the Belorussian and 1st Ukrainian Fronts which was named the 2nd Belorusssian Front. On March 4, 1944 the supreme command tasked the newly formed front with preparing and carrying out an offensive operation in order to advance the main forces to the Western Boug in the Brest-Gorodlo area, and move its main forces to the Pripyat’ in the Turov, Davyd-Gorodok, Stolits area. The main strike was then to be inflicted on Kovel, an important highway and railroad intersection.

[R013.FullName]
Liberation
[R013.Description]
By spring of 1944 the USSR had liberated almost its entire pre-war territory (with the exception of part of the Baltic Republics, Ukraine, and Byelorussia). Now it was time to start the liberation of the Eastern Europe. The Germans were largely demoralized by a series of defeats, the generals did not trust Hitler anymore, and as a consequence, an attempt was made to assassinate the dictator on July 20, 1944. The assassination failed and was followed by a series of repressions and reprisals throughout the Wehrmacht command. 

Meanwhile the Soviet command was preparing a large-scale offensive operation, which aimed at driving the enemy out of Ukraine and pushing them back from the eastern borders of Poland. The offensive was later named the Lvov-Sandomierz operation. The forces of the 1st Ukrainian and 1st Bellorussian Fronts were to attack the German group of Northern Ukraine. On the Russian side the operation involved up to 1.2 million soldiers, 13,900 guns and mortars (over 76mm), 2,200 tanks and self-propelled artillery units and 2,806 planes – all aiming at the Kolymya, Lutsk, Dubno area. 

The plan of the operation envisaged two powerful strikes that would break open the enemy defense lines in two locations, 60-70 km away from each other. The first strike was going to be inflicted from the area west of Lutsk in the direction of Sokal, Rawa-Russkaya while the second strike was going from Ternopol to Lvov in order to destroy the German group in Lvov, capture the city and the take over the fortress of Przemysl.

The operation began on July 13, 1944. Already by July 27 the Soviets managed to liberate Lvov. After that the forces of the 1st Ukrainian Front continued the advance onto the Vistula. Crossing the river was quite a difficult task: its width in the area of Sandomierz reached 250 meters, and the depth was over two meters. On July 29, the 3rd guard armies, in collaboration with the cavalry-mechanized group of General Sokolov, defeated the enemy at Annopol and reach the river. The next day the Russian advance divisions took over small bridgeheads to the north and south of Annopol. In August the enemy tried a series of powerful counterstrikes, in order to repulse which the Russians had to move in the 5th guard army from the reserve. The troops of the Ukrainian Front withheld all of the German counter-strikes and fortified themselves at a 75 x 60 km bridgehead.

[R014.FullName]
The Seelow Heights
[R014.Description]
The Nazi regime was in decay – in early 1945, following the defeat of the Germans in Eastern Prussia and Poland and after the Soviet troops finally invaded German territory; it was obvious that Hitler was doomed. Things had been no better on the Western Front following the defeat in the Ardennes. The Allies had crossed the Rhine and were pouring into Germany.  The war was not entering its very final phase. 

Hitler’s policy of the last five years was summarized in one of his favorite phrases: “I will not make any compromises and will never surrender!” His rich imagination and manic vanity caused him to underestimate the power of the Soviet Union and the resolve of the Western Allies. The early unstoppable successes on the Eastern Front finally ended in a complete defeat, and in spring of 1945 the troops of the 1st Bellorussian and 1st Ukrainian Fronts were standing before Berlin.

These were not the green troops that tried to resist the Wehrmacht onslaught in 1941: under trained under armed, and under the command of the generals whose experience – at best – was limited to the 1939 Finnish campaign or the Khalkhin-Gol operation. No, these were combat veterans that had fought during Moscow, Stalingrad and Kursk. They were armed with state-of-the-art weaponry and backed by the strongest aviation arm in the world and above all, they were driven with a burning desire for revenge and glory.

However, up until this point the Soviets had never had a chance of taking such a large and well-defended city as Berlin, occupying over 900 square kilometers in area. The staff of the 1st Bellorussian Front knew that Berlin was now basically a fortress-city, and that all of the Eastern approaches to it from the Oder were heavily defended.

The Russian intelligence service did everything it could to study the area and an exact scale model of the city was built and used both during the overall planning of the assault as well as the battle itself.
 
One of the most difficult areas for the advancing Soviet troops were the so-called Seelow highlands: a cross-country area, with many canals, high hills, and gullies and heavily defended by fanatical German troops that were prepared to fight to the bitter end. 

At 5 a.m. on April 16, the Russians began a massive artillery barrage of the area – 40,000 guns rained fire onto the German positions. It was the beginning of the end in Europe – the battle for Berlin.